{"kind":"propose","text":"Charter for the GitHub connector's outbound half on generalbusiness-ai/gitseq: pull requests and the connector's own comment. The inbound charter f696c18a scoped these out deliberately; this is the separate charter it promised.\n\nSCOPE. Opening pull requests on github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq, and editing one comment per pull request that the connector owns. Bound to this workroom, genesis 5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8. Same actor, `github-connector`, same key, same token-handed-to-the-process arrangement.\n\nTWO OUTBOUND THINGS, AND THEY ARE NOT ALIKE.\n\n**Rendering** is continuous and idempotent: the connector overwrites a comment it owns with the current state, stamped with the frontier it rendered. It deserves no ceremony, because overwriting a surface nothing reads back cannot conflict with anything. This is where the project's central claim lands on somebody else's interface — a pull request carrying a comment that reads *as of #4312 — STALE: the artifact this rested on was superseded* is the document that knows when it is wrong, sitting where people already look.\n\n**Opening a pull request** is a discrete side effect and goes through the work loop like any other work: the connector is requested, promises, acts on GitHub, and reports with the created pull request as evidence. Delivery failure then appears in the room as an unkept promise rather than as a silent error in a log nobody reads. The connector's own reliability becomes visible in the same terms as everyone else's, which is the point of having the loop.\n\nWHAT THE CONNECTOR MAY NOT DO. It may not approve. It may not merge. It may not ratify. gitseq's exact-head review and merge guard remain what decides whether a head lands; GitHub carries the conversation and the button. A pull request is a rendering surface, not an authority — and the connector holding no approval power is what keeps that true when somebody later wires the two together.\n\nNEVER READ BACK ITS OWN WRITING. The connector owns exactly the comment it edits in place, recognised by a marker it alone writes, and ingests nothing it wrote. Suppression by tagging is fragile because anyone can write a tag; ownership by structure is not. There is a second door here that only opens once this charter is live: the GitHub issues endpoint returns pull requests alongside issues, so the inbound half must keep excluding them or it will observe the outbound half's own output. That exclusion exists and is tested; this charter is the reason it matters.\n\nCREDENTIAL, ALREADY SETTLED. The process is handed a token and acts with whatever that token can do. Everything the connector writes to GitHub appears as the token's owner, so with hugh's token the pull requests will read as hughpyle. That is a property of the deployment, accepted rather than worked around: the gitseq log records that the connector acted, under which request, on whose behalf. The connector's reach is the token's reach, which is not the same bound as this charter's scope.\n\nWHAT THIS CHARTER DOES NOT DO. Detection, not prevention, exactly as before. The fold does not read charters. A connector whose key is stolen can state anything a connector may state, and one whose token is stolen can write anything that token can write — the second is a GitHub problem this charter cannot touch. What the record gives is attribution and containment after the fact."}